.TH SHEDIT 1 "2026-11-16" "shedit 1.0" "Terminal text input"
.SH NAME
shedit \- terminal text input
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shedit
[\fB\-bghox\fR]
[\fB\-r\fR \fIlines\fR]
[\fB\-p\fR \fItext\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shedit
is a terminal text input program. Unlike an editor, it does not use
the whole terminal. It opens at the cursor position and waits for
text input.
.PP
On submit, the whole text is printed to stdout.
.PP
On start, text can be preloaded from stdin. See
.BR EXAMPLES .
.PP
.B shedit
is meant to be a simple input program. It provides only minimal
editing with backspace and the cursor keys, though it is possible
to invoke the editor set in
.BR $EDITOR .
.PP
.B shedit
is part of the shui-tools collection.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-b
Display the bottom bar in reverse video.
.TP
.B \-g
Disable the bottom bar.
.TP
.B \-h
Show a short usage message.
.TP
.B \-o
Turn
.B shedit
into a single-line input. Enter submits the text, and all keys for
invoking the editor or inserting a newline are disabled.
\er and \en are stripped from stdin input.
.TP
\fB\-p\fR \fItext\fR
Set the bottom bar text.
.TP
\fB\-r\fR \fIlines\fR
Define the height of the input field in lines.
0 uses the whole terminal. The default is 3.
.TP
.B \-x
By default, CTRL-D or ESC d submit the text. With
.BR \-x ,
Enter acts as a submit key.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B CTRL-J
Insert a newline.
.TP
\fBCTRL-E\fR or \fBESC e\fR
Open the editor configured in
.BR $EDITOR .
.TP
\fBCTRL-X\fR or \fBESC\fR or \fBESC q\fR or \fBESC x\fR
Abort without saving the text and exit with status 1.
.TP
\fBCTRL-D\fR or \fBESC d\fR
Submit the text and exit with status 0.
.SH EXAMPLES
Wait for a message to be typed:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
chat=$(shedit)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Preload intro.txt with a height of 10 lines:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
chat=$(shedit \-r10 <intro.txt)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Edit a stream before saving:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
jq '.[]' file.json | shedit \-r 20 >new.json
.fi
.RE
.PP
Edit a filename:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
new_name=$(printf "filename.txt" | shedit \-o)
.fi
.RE
.SH BUGS
Probably.
